# powerarm.install -- binfmt_misc re-registration, and the first-run message.
#
# THE IMPORTANT PART IS _powerarm_rebind, AND IT RUNS ON UPGRADE TOO.
#
# /usr/lib/binfmt.d/POWERarm-aarch64.conf registers with the flags POCF.  F
# ("fix binary") makes the kernel open /usr/bin/POWERarm when the entry is
# registered and hold THAT INODE for the life of the entry.  An upgrade writes
# a new /usr/bin/POWERarm at a new inode; the old one stays pinned and open, so
# without a re-registration every aarch64 process on the machine keeps running
# the emulator from BEFORE the upgrade.  The entry still prints
# "interpreter /usr/bin/POWERarm", and the kernel passes that same string as
# argv[0], so neither the registration, nor `ps`, nor the process's own argv
# gives any sign of it.  Somebody upgrades to get a fix, does not get the fix,
# and has nothing to look at.
#
# `systemctl restart` and not `start`: the unit's ExecStop is
# `systemd-binfmt --unregister`, and only that teardown releases the pinned
# inode.  ExecStart on its own hits EEXIST on an entry of the same name, logs a
# warning, and leaves the stale registration exactly where it was.
#
# post_remove restarts it as well, so the entry disappears with the package
# rather than outliving it as a handler for a file that is no longer there.

_powerarm_rebind() {
  systemctl restart systemd-binfmt.service 2>/dev/null || {
    cat <<'EOF'
powerarm: could not restart systemd-binfmt.service.

  The AArch64 binfmt_misc handler is stale or absent until it is re-registered:

    sudo systemctl restart systemd-binfmt.service

  Do not use `start`: only the unit's stop step unregisters the old entry, and
  the old entry pins the OLD emulator's inode.
EOF
  }
}

post_install() {
  _powerarm_rebind
  cat <<'EOF'

POWERarm installed.  aarch64 binaries now run directly, through binfmt_misc.

  NEXT STEP -- you do not have a guest root filesystem yet, and without one
  nothing useful runs.  POWERarm resolves guest paths inside an AArch64 rootfs;
  with none, lookups fall back to the host tree silently and a guest binary
  dies on the host's ppc64le loader with no useful error.

  Build one (no root needed, ~600 MB installed, ~120 MB downloaded, needs
  network, python, gpg/gpgv and zstd):

    /usr/share/powerarm/rootfs/build-alarm-sysroot.sh

  It fetches pinned Arch Linux ARM packages, checks every sha256 and every
  signature against the pinned Arch Linux ARM key, and extracts them to
  ~/.local/share/powerarm/RootFS/ArchLinuxARM-m2 -- which is the rootfs this
  package's /usr/share/powerarm/Config.json already selects.  Nothing to edit.

  Then:

    POWERarm /usr/bin/uname -m           # prints aarch64
    ~/.local/share/powerarm/RootFS/ArchLinuxARM-m2/usr/bin/uname -m
                                         # the same, via binfmt_misc

  Do NOT use POWERarmRootFSFetcher: it is inherited from FEX and downloads
  x86_64/i386 images, which are useless here.

  Per-user settings live in ~/.config/powerarm/Config.json (POWERarmConfig is
  the GUI for it) and override the system defaults.

  If you also run a development build from ~/.local/opt/powerarm-stable, read
  /usr/share/doc/powerarm/README.md: /etc/binfmt.d masks /usr/lib/binfmt.d, so
  the hand-registered one keeps winning.  To see which emulator really handles
  your binaries:

    bash /usr/share/powerarm/check-binfmt-inode.sh

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  _powerarm_rebind
  cat <<'EOF'

POWERarm upgraded, and the binfmt_misc entry was re-registered against the new
binary.  If the restart above failed, every aarch64 process is still running
the PREVIOUS emulator; nothing will say so.  Confirm with:

  bash /usr/share/powerarm/check-binfmt-inode.sh

Long-running guests started before the upgrade keep the old emulator until they
exit -- that is the pinned inode, and it is fine.  Restart them to pick this up.

EOF
}

post_remove() {
  _powerarm_rebind
  cat <<'EOF'

POWERarm removed and its binfmt_misc entry unregistered.  Guest root
filesystems under ~/.local/share/powerarm/ are left alone; delete them by hand
if you want the space back.

EOF
}
